F-1174 (J-0795): while auditing whether anything the "NATURE STUDY lesson" try's else branch (nodes/inner_monologue_node.py) reaches can raise NonLiveWriteError, a genuinely new, previously-unnamed connecting-tissue swallow was found by direct read one layer further down, in core/nature_study.py itself -- confirmed, not assumed, and not fixed here (a different file than either of J-0795's own two named targets, matching this plan's own repeated precedent of spawning rather than folding in a genuinely new site found while auditing an adjacent one, e.g. J-0768's/J-0782's own identical precedent).

The gap: core/nature_study.py's run() (line 35, async, the observation lesson's own entry point) calls _scene() (line 20) as its own first statement. _scene()'s full body:

    def _scene() -> str:
        try:
            from core import sensorium
            loc = sensorium.where()
            if loc == "garden":
                return sensorium.look("garden") or sensorium.look("sea")
            if loc == "shore":
                return sensorium.look("pools") or sensorium.look("shore")
            return sensorium.look("window") or sensorium.scene_line()
        except Exception:
            return "your garden on its hill above the sea"

sensorium.look() and sensorium.scene_line() are BOTH already fully fenced end-to-end (confirmed by direct read, this job): look()'s own inner call to life.look_answer() re-raises NonLiveWriteError itself (J-0751, "except profile.NonLiveWriteError: raise" ahead of look()'s own final "except Exception:"), and scene_line() carries the identical two-layer carve-out for its own inner locomotion.pending_line()/spatial.render() calls (J-0778/J-0794) ahead of its own final "except Exception:". So both CAN genuinely raise NonLiveWriteError up to their own caller today. But _scene()'s own bare "except Exception: return \"...\"" (no NonLiveWriteError carve-out) sits directly around every one of these calls -- so if either ever actually raised non-live, THIS function would silently swallow it and return the fallback string, one layer inside nature_study.py itself, before the raise could reach run()'s own return, let alone whatever called run() (nodes/inner_monologue_node.py's own "NATURE STUDY lesson" try, J-0795, already carries its own correct carve-out one layer further out -- but that carve-out cannot see a raise this function already ate).

sensorium.where() (also called in _scene(), unconditionally, before the branch) is confirmed NOT itself raise-capable: it calls _load() only when the module-level `state` dict is still empty, and _load() (core/sensorium.py line 102) does not call guard_path() anywhere (read in full) -- it only reads and sets in-memory defaults, no write path at all. So where() is provably inert; the two look()/scene_line() calls are the real, confirmed-live gap.

Scope for the spawned job:
1. Add "except profile.NonLiveWriteError: raise" to _scene()'s own try, before its existing "except Exception:", matching this fence's established placement. Re-locate the exact current line numbers by direct read/grep first (do not trust this briefing's own line citations -- other jobs in this same batch chain, and jobs downstream of it, may land on nearby files first).
2. Check whether _scene()'s own caller (run(), line 35) or anything else in run()/`_record()` needs a matching carve-out of its own once _scene() can raise. This job's own read (F-1174) already found `scene = _scene()` sits as run()'s own first statement, with NO wrapping try of its own around it -- run()'s only try starts on the NEXT statement (around the `call_json(...)` LLM call) -- so on today's structure this looks like a ONE-layer fix (only inside _scene() itself), the same shape practical_lesson.run() already has for _current_task(), NOT the "both layers needed" shape J-0778 found for sensorium.scene_line() and J-0782/J-0795 found for inner_monologue_node.py's own outer school/nature tries. Re-confirm by reading run()'s actual current source before trusting this -- do not assume either way just because this briefing says so.
3. Verify empirically in a throwaway git worktree (never git stash/reset/checkout -- <path> in any shared tree): monkeypatch sensorium.look/sensorium.scene_line to raise profile.NonLiveWriteError directly, call the real _scene() (or nature_study.run()) before/after -- NO_RAISE pre-fix -> RAISED post-fix -- plus a benign case (ordinary string return) under both profile.LIVE=False/True, confirming the everyday observation-lesson path is unaffected.
4. Re-run scripts/life_smoke.py and scripts/_test_dispatch_family_scope.py clean afterward (the narrowest regression surface that already covers this plan's own isolation-proof convention); check whether a dedicated nature_study/observation-lesson test script exists and run it too if so.

